  </section>
  <section id="ballooning" class="g38-sec fai-sec">
    <h2>Ballooning</h2>
<p>Ballooning means putting a unique number on every design characteristic in the design documents, so each one can be traced to a line on Form 3. Rev C defines the FAIR as including every ballooned design characteristic, so the ballooned documents are part of the package. A missed balloon is a missed requirement, and it is one of the most common reasons an FAIR comes back.</p>
<h3 id="what-to-balloon">What to balloon</h3>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>Every dimension</strong>, including angles, radii, chamfers and thread callouts.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Every geometric tolerance</strong> (each feature control frame).</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Every note</strong>, general and flag notes alike. A note with several separate requirements may need several balloons.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Title block requirements:</strong> material, finish, default tolerances that apply to untoleranced dimensions, surface finish, weight, part marking.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Parts list entries</strong> for assemblies, and any other document the drawing points to that carries requirements, such as a wire list, a separate parts list, a data sheet, or requirements on the purchase order.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Specifications called out</strong> on the drawing, especially processes such as plating, heat treat or painting.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Characteristics in a 3D model</strong> that aren't shown on a 2D sheet. When the design is a digital product definition, the needed characteristics are extracted from the model and ballooned on a document like any other.</p></li>
</ul>
<p>Leave out nothing because it is "obvious." Edge breaks, deburr notes and marking requirements are characteristics too.</p>
<h3 id="good-ballooning-practice">Good ballooning practice</h3>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>One number, one requirement.</strong> Never give the same characteristic two balloons, on the same document or across two documents. The duplicate makes reviewers ask which result counts.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Number in a clear order</strong>, for example sheet by sheet, left to right, top to bottom, so a reviewer can find each balloon.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Put the balloon right beside what it covers</strong>, with a leader if the area is crowded.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Account for excluded characteristics.</strong> If the PO says the part ships without a feature (to be finished by the customer, for example), balloon it anyway and note why it isn't inspected.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Balloon reference dimensions only if you want to.</strong> Rev C counts basic dimensions as reference characteristics too. If you balloon them, their result is N/A.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Check the ballooned documents match the revision</strong> on Form 1 fields 5–7, sheet for sheet.</p></li>
</ul>
<p>Ballooning software can number characteristics and build Form 3 automatically. It is still your job to check that nothing was skipped and nothing was counted twice.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="assemblies" class="g38-sec fai-sec">
    <h2>Assemblies, harnesses and wire lists</h2>
<p>An assembly FAI verifies what the assembly drawing adds, and accounts for everything that went into it. The parts inside are covered by their own FAIRs or certificates, referenced from the assembly's Form 1.</p>
<div class="fai-tree" role="img" aria-label="Every item in an assembly traces to its own record">
  <div class="fai-tree-title">Every item in an assembly traces to its own record</div>
  <div class="fai-tree-root"><b>Assembly FAIR</b><span>Forms 1&ndash;3; every BOM item on the Form 1 index</span></div>
  <div class="fai-tree-kids">
    <div><b>Sub-assembly</b><span>Its own FAIR<br>FAIR ID on the index</span></div>
    <div><b>Detail part</b><span>Its own FAIR<br>FAIR ID on the index</span></div>
    <div><b>COTS / hardware</b><span>No FAIR if unmodified<br>C of C or PO on index</span></div>
    <div><b>Materials</b><span>Wire, bar stock, etc.<br>Certs on Form 2</span></div>
  </div>
</div>
<h3 id="the-parts-index-form-1-fields-1518">The parts index (Form 1, fields 15–18)</h3>
<p>List every item on the bill of materials: detail parts, sub-assemblies, hardware, COTS parts and software. Materials traced on Form 2, such as wire, sleeving or adhesive, don't need repeating on Form 1. For each one, give its part type and a traceable reference:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>Detail parts and sub-assemblies</strong> you or a supplier made to a drawing: their own FAIR identifier.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Standard catalogue and COTS items:</strong> no FAIR needed. Many customers want a C of C, PO or lot reference so the item can be traced.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Materials:</strong> trace them on Form 2, with their certs.</p></li>
</ul>
<p>Check that the BOM revision you worked from matches the drawing revision on fields 5–7, and that nothing on the BOM is missing from the index.</p>
<p>Detail part features created or changed during assembly (holes drilled at assembly, for example) can be verified on the assembly FAI. Everything else about a detail part belongs on its own FAIR, which the assembly FAIR can reference.</p>
<h3 id="harness-and-wire-list-fais">Harness and wire-list FAIs</h3>
<p>Wire harnesses and cable assemblies carry most of their requirements in tables: a parts list of wire types and a wire list (or run list) of every connection. The ballooning rules still apply. Every requirement gets one unique number, and nothing is counted twice.</p>
<p>A typical setup, and how to balloon it:</p>
<ol type="1">
<li><p><strong>The drawing callout.</strong> Drawings often say only "install items 100 thru 154 per wire list." Give that note one balloon. Its result points to the wire list balloons, for example: "Accept, see chars 40 thru 94."</p></li>
<li><p><strong>The wire list.</strong> Balloon each run once, on the wire list. That one balloon covers the whole row: the wire's find number, the from and to terminations, and any length, color, gauge or marking the row specifies. Write all of it into the Form 3 requirement: "Find No. 112, J1-3 to P2-7, 18.0 in."</p></li>
<li><p><strong>The parts list.</strong> Balloon each wire type on the parts list. These verify the material: the right wire specification, gauge and color, with its cert on Form 2.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Test results.</strong> Continuity, insulation resistance or hi-pot tests the drawing calls for go on Form 2 as functional tests, with the test report attached.</p></li>
</ol>
<p>Don't balloon each run again on the drawing when the drawing doesn't show run detail. That gives each wire two numbers, and a reviewer will ask which result counts.</p>
<p>When a wire is wrong, list that run on its own Form 3 line with what was found, like any other nonconforming characteristic.</p>
<p>AS9102 doesn't address wire lists specifically, and customers' requirements vary. Some want every run on Form 3; others accept a referenced wire list or test report with per-run results. Check the customer's requirements first.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="ncr" class="g38-sec fai-sec">
    <h2>Nonconformances</h2>
<p>A failed characteristic doesn't stop you from finishing the FAIR, but it has to be documented and then fixed. A FAIR with an open nonconformance has not proven the process.</p>
<p>When something fails during the FAI:</p>
<ol type="1">
<li><p><strong>Record it on Form 3</strong> on its own line, with the actual measured value.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Open a nonconformance report (NCR)</strong> under your normal nonconforming product procedure, and enter its number in Form 3, field 11.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Check "Yes" on Form 1, field 19.</strong></p></li>
<li><p><strong>Contain the parts.</strong> Other parts in the lot may have the same problem; hold and inspect them before anything ships.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Disposition the NCR.</strong> AS9102 doesn't cover disposition; your nonconforming product procedure does. On customer-designed parts, a "use as is" or "repair" decision usually needs the customer's approval.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Fix the cause.</strong> Correct the process, tooling, program or paperwork that produced the failure.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Re-verify.</strong> Do a partial FAI on a conforming part for the characteristics that failed (and any the fix could affect), using the original FAI as the baseline. A full FAI is also acceptable.</p></li>
</ol>
<p>Once every nonconformance has been corrected and verified, Rev C says to check "No" in Form 1, field 19. Characteristics the nonconformance didn't affect stay valid, even if the FAI part itself is scrapped. Someone reading the records should be able to follow the trail from the failure, to the NCR, to the FAI that proved the fix.</p>
<p>A "No" in Form 2, field 9 (an unapproved source where approval is required) is a nonconformance too, handled the same way.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="review" class="g38-sec fai-sec">
    <h2>Reviewing an FAIR</h2>
<p>Reviewing an FAIR means checking the paperwork as carefully as the part. Most FAIR problems are in the documents: a missed note, a cert that doesn't match, a revision that isn't the one on the PO. Hold your own FAIRs and supplier FAIRs to the same standard.</p>
<h3 id="reviewers-checklist">Reviewer's checklist</h3>
<p><strong>Start with the paperwork</strong></p>
<ul class="fai-check">
<li><label><input type="checkbox" />Read the PO and its quality clauses: is an FAI required, and does the customer add any requirements?</label></li>
<li><label><input type="checkbox" />Confirm the drawing, parts list and other design documents are the revisions the PO calls for</label></li>
<li><label><input type="checkbox" />Check for engineering orders, deviations or waivers that should appear in Form 1, field 8</label></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Form 1</strong></p>
<ul class="fai-check">
<li><label><input type="checkbox" />Part number, name, revision and drawing revision match the PO and the drawing</label></li>
<li><label><input type="checkbox" />FAIR identifier is filled in and the same on all three forms</label></li>
<li><label><input type="checkbox" />Detail/assembly and full/partial are checked; a reason is given; a partial FAI names its baseline</label></li>
<li><label><input type="checkbox" />For assemblies, every BOM item is in the index with a part type and a traceable reference</label></li>
<li><label><input type="checkbox" />Field 19 matches Form 3: "Yes" if any characteristic failed</label></li>
<li><label><input type="checkbox" />Fields 20 and 22 are signed and dated by two different people</label></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Form 2</strong></p>
<ul class="fai-check">
<li><label><input type="checkbox" />Every material, special process and functional test on the drawing, parts list and PO is listed</label></li>
<li><label><input type="checkbox" />Each line has a cert or report, and the cert matches the spec, class, condition and lot</label></li>
<li><label><input type="checkbox" />Special process sources are approved where the customer requires it</label></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Form 3 and the ballooned drawing</strong></p>
<ul class="fai-check">
<li><label><input type="checkbox" />Every dimension, note and title block requirement has a balloon, and every balloon has a Form 3 line</label></li>
<li><label><input type="checkbox" />No characteristic is ballooned twice</label></li>
<li><label><input type="checkbox" />Requirements include their tolerances</label></li>
<li><label><input type="checkbox" />Variable data has measured values, not just "accept"</label></li>
<li><label><input type="checkbox" />Every result is within its tolerance; anything out has its own line and an NCR number</label></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>The package</strong></p>
<ul class="fai-check">
<li><label><input type="checkbox" />All three forms are present</label></li>
<li><label><input type="checkbox" />Every referenced cert, report and sub-tier FAIR is attached</label></li>
<li><label><input type="checkbox" />Everything is legible</label></li>
</ul>
<h3 id="common-reasons-fairs-are-rejected">Common reasons FAIRs are rejected</h3>
<p>Customer guides list much the same problems again and again:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>missing FAIRs or test reports for sub-assemblies and detail parts</p></li>
<li><p>characteristics not accounted for: an unballooned note, a missed title block requirement</p></li>
<li><p>"Accept" or "Pass" recorded for a dimension with numerical limits</p></li>
<li><p>tolerances missing from Form 3</p></li>
<li><p>certs not included, or not matching what Form 2 says</p></li>
<li><p>special processor name, address or approval missing from Form 2</p></li>
<li><p>missing signatures or dates</p></li>
<li><p>the customer's added requirements not followed</p></li>
<li><p>a missing form, or entries that can't be read</p></li>
</ul>
  </section>
  <section id="revc" class="g38-sec fai-sec">
    <h2>Rev A/B vs Rev C numbering</h2>
<p>AS9102 Rev C was published in June 2023 and renumbered the end of Form 1. Many customer guides, training slides and older forms still use Rev A/B numbers, so "Box 19" can mean two different things. Always check which revision a form or instruction is written to.</p>
<h3 id="form-1">Form 1</h3>
<p>Fields 1–16 keep their numbers. From 17 on:</p>
<div class="g38-scroll"><table class="ref-table">
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th>Field</th>
<th>Rev A/B</th>
<th>Rev C</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td>17</td>
<td>Part Serial Number (index)</td>
<td>Part Type (index)</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>18</td>
<td>FAIR Number (index)</td>
<td>FAIR Identifier (index)</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>19</td>
<td>Signature, plus "FAI Complete / Not Complete"</td>
<td>Does FAIR Contain a Documented Nonconformance(s)?</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>20</td>
<td>Date</td>
<td>FAIR Verified By</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>21</td>
<td>Reviewed By (optional)</td>
<td>Date</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>22</td>
<td>Date</td>
<td>FAIR Reviewed / Approved By (required)</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>23</td>
<td>Customer Approval</td>
<td>Date</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>24</td>
<td>Date</td>
<td>Customer Approval</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>25</td>
<td>none</td>
<td>Date</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>26</td>
<td>none</td>
<td>Comments</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Other Form 1 changes in Rev C: field 4 (FAIR identifier) became required, and field 14 now needs a reason for a full FAI as well as a partial one.</p>
<h3 id="forms-2-and-3">Forms 2 and 3</h3>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>Form 2:</strong> the "prepared by" signature and date at the bottom were removed. Field 4 became required.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Form 3:</strong> the signature and date were removed, and the comments column became field 12 (it was 14). Field 4 became required, and field 8 now covers software part numbers and revisions.</p></li>
</ul>
<p>In Rev C, Form 1 alone carries the sign-offs for the whole package.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="glossary" class="g38-sec fai-sec">
    <h2>Glossary</h2>
<div class="g38-scroll"><table class="ref-table">
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th>Term</th>
<th>Meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td>AS9102</td>
<td>The aerospace standard for FAI documentation, published by SAE International (also issued as EN 9102 and JISQ 9102). Current revision: C</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Assembly</td>
<td>A product made of other parts, with a bill of materials</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Attribute data</td>
<td>A result recorded as accept or reject, for requirements with no numerical limits</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Baseline part number</td>
<td>The part number and revision of the approved FAI a partial FAI is measured against</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Ballooning</td>
<td>Numbering each design characteristic on the design documents so it traces to Form 3</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>BOM</td>
<td>Bill of materials: the list of everything that goes into an assembly</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>C of C</td>
<td>Certificate of conformance: a supplier's signed statement that material or work meets requirements</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>COTS</td>
<td>Commercial off-the-shelf: an item sold to the public, defined by its maker or a standard, not modified for a specific customer</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Design characteristic</td>
<td>Any feature or requirement that defines the product: a dimension, tolerance, note, material, process, test or marking</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Designed tooling</td>
<td>A gauge, fixture or NC program made specifically for one part and used to inspect it</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Detail part</td>
<td>A single piece part, not made of other parts</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>DPD</td>
<td>Digital product definition: a 3D model or data set that defines the product, alone or with 2D drawings</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>FAI</td>
<td>First Article Inspection</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>FAIR</td>
<td>First Article Inspection Report: the forms and evidence for one FAI</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Find number</td>
<td>The item number that ties a component in a drawing or wire list to its line in the parts list</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>First production run</td>
<td>The first parts made with the process that will be used for ongoing production</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Full FAI</td>
<td>An FAI covering every requirement of the part</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Key characteristic</td>
<td>A feature whose variation significantly affects fit, performance or service life, marked for special control</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Modified COTS / standard item</td>
<td>A COTS or standard catalogue item that has been altered (drilled, cut, plated). It needs an FAI</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Multiple characteristics</td>
<td>Identical features at several locations set by one requirement, such as "4X" holes</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Nadcap</td>
<td>The aerospace industry's accreditation program for special process suppliers</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>NCR</td>
<td>Nonconformance report: the record of a failed requirement and its disposition</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Partial (delta) FAI</td>
<td>An FAI covering only the characteristics affected by a change</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Qualified tooling</td>
<td>General-purpose, calibrated gauges such as go/no-go, thread or radius gauges</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Reference characteristic</td>
<td>A dimension shown for information only, without a tolerance. Rev C includes basic dimensions</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Special process</td>
<td>A process whose result can't be fully checked afterward, such as heat treat, plating or welding</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Standard catalogue item</td>
<td>A part fully defined by an industry, national or military standard, such as standard fasteners</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Variable data</td>
<td>A result recorded as a measured value on a continuous scale</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
  </section>
  <section id="sources" class="g38-sec fai-sec">
    <h2>Official sources and further reading</h2>
<p>The standard itself is the final word. Buy a copy for anyone who prepares or approves FAIRs.</p>
<ul>
<li><p><a href="https://webstore.ansi.org/standards/sae/sae9102c2023" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SAE AS9102C (2023), Aerospace Series – First Article Inspection Requirements</a>: the standard, sold by SAE through ANSI and other distributors</p></li>
<li><p><a href="https://iaqg.org/standard/9102-first-article-inspection-requirement/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IAQG 9102 standard page</a>: IAQG's page for the standard, with links to its guidance</p></li>
<li><p><a href="https://ma.iaqg.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/9102_Changes_07112023.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IAQG: 9102 key changes from Rev B to Rev C</a>: the official list of what changed, field by field</p></li>
<li><p><a href="https://ma.iaqg.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/9102-FAQ-1.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IAQG: 9102 frequently asked questions</a>: written for Rev B; IAQG says the guidance has since been folded into Rev C and the SCMH</p></li>
<li><p><a href="https://www.net-inspect.com/blog/as9102-rev-b-vs-rev-c/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Net-Inspect: AS9102 Rev B vs Rev C</a> and <a href="https://www.discussoftware.com/news/as9102-rev-c-what-you-need-to-know/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DISCUS: AS9102 Rev C, what you need to know</a>: two software vendors' summaries of the Rev C changes</p></li>
</ul>
<p>The IAQG Supply Chain Management Handbook (SCMH) section on 9102 has further guidance, including the FAI report completion guide. Customers' own supplier FAI requirements, usually linked from their supplier portals, add to all of the above.</p>
  </section>
</div>
